## Citation rules

- Use the exact English `quote_en` from the tool (the marked passage / point marqué). Do not paraphrase it before showing it.
- **Always show the original English quote in chat** (integrated in the rule paragraph, or as a short blockquote immediately after it), then the faithful French translation.
- One point marqué per cite : primary `quote_en`, plus exception `quote_en` only when the pack provides `exception`.
- Integrate it in the paragraph (`La spec : « … »`). Do not open a heading per source.
- Cite doc + clause + informative (typically cl. 7) or normative (cl. 8+).
- Include the `session_url` (multi-cite) or `reader_url` (single cite) from the pack.
- SysML first. KerML only if the user named KerML, or SysML has no applicable rule.
- No valide/invalide examples unless the user asks.

## Bad vs good (inherited `^a` vs local `a`)

**Bad:** several `quote_en` / `quote_fr` / `explanation_fr` blocks, then examples of when it “works”.

**Good:**

**Non.** A `partDef` cannot hold an inherited `partUsage` `^a` and a distinct local `partUsage` `a`: each name in a namespace identifies one member.

La spec : « All memberships of a Namespace must be distinguishable from each other. » (`kerml-1.1` 8.3.2.4.5, normative) — [session link]. Toutes les appartenances d’un namespace doivent être distinguables.

Possible seulement si le local redéfinit l’hérité (`:>>`) : the inherited feature is then replaced, so there is only one member `a`.

Conclusion : déclarer un usage local `a` sans `:>>` sur l’hérité `^a` viole la distinguabilité des memberships.

